WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.694
Appraisal Discussion.

2
00:00:01.974 --> 00:00:05.814
Day 88 of the Soft Skill Playbook course, in the Career phase.

3
00:00:07.564 --> 00:00:08.588
The key idea.

4
00:00:08.868 --> 00:00:14.564
The appraisal is where your rating turns into money: the hike, the bonus, sometimes a new title.

5
00:00:14.844 --> 00:00:22.737
Find out how the numbers were decided, respond with data rather than emotion, and leave with either an improvement or a written plan.

6
00:00:24.087 --> 00:00:24.855
Info:

7
00:00:25.135 --> 00:00:27.311
Appraisal versus performance review.

8
00:00:27.591 --> 00:00:30.493
The Performance Review is about feedback and rating.

9
00:00:30.773 --> 00:00:37.514
The appraisal discussion is about the outcome: hike %, bonus, promotion, and what happens if you disagree.

10
00:00:37.794 --> 00:00:41.378
In many companies they happen in the same meeting; prepare for both.

11
00:00:42.728 --> 00:00:43.581
Part 1.

12
00:00:43.861 --> 00:00:45.077
Before the discussion.

13
00:00:46.327 --> 00:00:47.245
Step 1:

14
00:00:47.525 --> 00:00:52.453
Know the system: rating scale, hike bands, bonus formula, promotion cycle.

15
00:00:52.733 --> 00:00:53.650
Step 2:

16
00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:58.282
Know your market: what your role pays outside (see Salary Negotiation).

17
00:00:58.562 --> 00:00:59.479
Step 3:

18
00:00:59.759 --> 00:01:03.535
Know your case: top 3 to 5 wins with numbers (your wins file).

19
00:01:03.815 --> 00:01:04.775
Step 4:

20
00:01:05.055 --> 00:01:08.319
Know your ask: the specific hike or title you think is fair.

21
00:01:08.599 --> 00:01:09.559
Step 5:

22
00:01:09.839 --> 00:01:12.869
Know your floor: what you'll do if the outcome is below it.

23
00:01:14.219 --> 00:01:17.184
Questions to research (or ask HR) beforehand.

24
00:01:17.464 --> 00:01:19.789
How are hike percentages linked to ratings?

25
00:01:20.069 --> 00:01:21.627
Is there a range per rating?

26
00:01:21.907 --> 00:01:25.811
Is there a separate market correction budget for people paid below band?

27
00:01:26.091 --> 00:01:30.827
How is the variable bonus calculated (company %, individual %)?

28
00:01:31.107 --> 00:01:34.669
When is the promotion cycle, and is promotion a separate hike?

29
00:01:34.949 --> 00:01:37.424
Can a rating or hike be reviewed or appealed?

30
00:01:37.704 --> 00:01:38.728
Who decides?

31
00:01:40.078 --> 00:01:40.953
Part 2.

32
00:01:41.233 --> 00:01:42.854
Understanding your hike letter.

33
00:01:44.104 --> 00:01:45.469
Here is a quick table:

34
00:01:45.749 --> 00:01:47.307
Item, and Ask yourself.

35
00:01:47.587 --> 00:01:49.187
Fixed pay increase %:

36
00:01:49.467 --> 00:01:54.907
Is it above or below inflation (~5 to 6%) and the company average?

37
00:01:55.187 --> 00:01:56.723
New CTC breakdown:

38
00:01:57.003 --> 00:01:59.947
Did fixed go up, or only variable or benefits?

39
00:02:00.227 --> 00:02:01.571
Variable or bonus:

40
00:02:01.851 --> 00:02:04.133
Target versus actual payout %?

41
00:02:04.413 --> 00:02:05.480
When is it paid?

42
00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:07.083
ESOPs or RSUs:

43
00:02:07.363 --> 00:02:08.237
New grant?

44
00:02:08.517 --> 00:02:09.712
Vesting schedule?

45
00:02:09.992 --> 00:02:11.059
Effective date:

46
00:02:11.339 --> 00:02:12.917
Is it backdated (arrears)?

47
00:02:13.197 --> 00:02:14.371
Title or level:

48
00:02:14.651 --> 00:02:16.933
Did your level change, or just the title?

49
00:02:18.283 --> 00:02:19.030
Warning:

50
00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:20.889
Watch the CTC trick.

51
00:02:21.169 --> 00:02:28.955
A 15% CTC hike can mean only 8% more fixed pay if the rest is variable, insurance or gratuity.

52
00:02:29.235 --> 00:02:32.499
Always compare monthly in-hand and fixed pay year on year.

53
00:02:33.849 --> 00:02:34.745
Part 3.

54
00:02:35.025 --> 00:02:35.815
Scripts.

55
00:02:37.065 --> 00:02:38.003
Good outcome.

56
00:02:38.283 --> 00:02:39.222
You could say:

57
00:02:39.502 --> 00:02:42.574
Thank you, I really appreciate this and the recognition.

58
00:02:42.854 --> 00:02:44.582
I'd like to keep this momentum.

59
00:02:44.862 --> 00:02:48.766
What should I focus on so I'm considered for promotion in the next cycle?

60
00:02:50.116 --> 00:02:51.673
Hike below expectation.

61
00:02:51.953 --> 00:02:52.892
You could say:

62
00:02:53.172 --> 00:02:54.921
Thank you for walking me through this.

63
00:02:55.201 --> 00:02:59.895
I'll be honest: the hike is lower than I expected given my rating and impact.

64
00:03:00.175 --> 00:03:05.444
This year I led the checkout migration and cut cloud costs by 35 lakh rupees.

65
00:03:05.724 --> 00:03:08.711
Could you help me understand how the number was decided?

66
00:03:10.061 --> 00:03:10.765
Then:

67
00:03:12.115 --> 00:03:13.053
You could say:

68
00:03:13.333 --> 00:03:18.069
Based on market data for my role, I'm currently about 15% below the range.

69
00:03:18.349 --> 00:03:23.149
Is there a possibility of a market correction, either now or at the mid-year review?

70
00:03:24.499 --> 00:03:27.486
Manager says \It's out of my hands\.

71
00:03:27.766 --> 00:03:28.705
You could say:

72
00:03:28.985 --> 00:03:31.161
I understand budgets are set centrally.

73
00:03:31.441 --> 00:03:35.089
Could you take my case to HR or your manager for a correction?

74
00:03:35.369 --> 00:03:38.739
I'm happy to put the details in writing so it's easy to share.

75
00:03:39.019 --> 00:03:44.801
And if nothing can change now, could we agree in writing on a review in 6 months with clear criteria?

76
00:03:46.151 --> 00:03:47.921
Promised promotion didn't happen.

77
00:03:48.201 --> 00:03:49.140
You could say:

78
00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:54.583
Last cycle we agreed that leading the platform migration would make the case for promotion.

79
00:03:54.863 --> 00:03:56.463
I delivered it in September.

80
00:03:56.743 --> 00:03:57.703
What changed?

81
00:03:57.983 --> 00:04:03.508
I'd like to understand the gap, and get a specific timeline and criteria in writing this time.

82
00:04:04.858 --> 00:04:06.501
Rating and hike don't match.

83
00:04:06.781 --> 00:04:07.719
You could say:

84
00:04:07.999 --> 00:04:12.757
My rating is 'Exceeds Expectations', but the hike is close to the company average.

85
00:04:13.037 --> 00:04:16.301
Is the hike band for my rating different from what I understood?

86
00:04:16.581 --> 00:04:19.525
I want to make sure there's no error before we close this.

87
00:04:20.875 --> 00:04:22.731
Mistakes do happen in the system.

88
00:04:23.011 --> 00:04:24.589
Asking politely is fine.

89
00:04:25.939 --> 00:04:27.945
Asking for non-cash alternatives.

90
00:04:28.225 --> 00:04:29.163
You could say:

91
00:04:29.443 --> 00:04:37.401
If the fixed hike can't change, could we look at a one-time bonus, an extra ESOP grant, a certification budget, or a title change?

92
00:04:37.681 --> 00:04:40.454
Any of these would show recognition of this year's work.

93
00:04:41.804 --> 00:04:42.721
Part 4.

94
00:04:43.001 --> 00:04:46.329
Escalating properly (if the outcome is clearly unfair).

95
00:04:47.579 --> 00:04:48.561
Sleep on it.

96
00:04:48.841 --> 00:04:50.867
Don't react in the meeting or on Slack.

97
00:04:51.147 --> 00:04:53.558
Ask your manager for the reasoning in writing.

98
00:04:53.838 --> 00:04:57.209
Write a calm, factual email with your achievements and data.

99
00:04:57.489 --> 00:05:01.521
Use the official review or appeal process (HR, skip-level).

100
00:05:01.801 --> 00:05:03.102
Decide on your floor.

101
00:05:03.382 --> 00:05:06.539
If it isn't met, start planning quietly and professionally.

102
00:05:07.889 --> 00:05:09.767
Here is a template you can copy.

103
00:05:10.047 --> 00:05:10.921
Subject:

104
00:05:11.201 --> 00:05:13.484
Request to review my appraisal outcome.

105
00:05:13.764 --> 00:05:14.681
Hi Priya.

106
00:05:14.961 --> 00:05:16.839
Thank you for our discussion today.

107
00:05:17.119 --> 00:05:21.279
As mentioned, I'd like to request a review of my. appraisal outcome.

108
00:05:21.559 --> 00:05:22.327
Summary:

109
00:05:22.607 --> 00:05:23.417
Rating:

110
00:05:23.697 --> 00:05:25.191
Exceeds Expectations.

111
00:05:25.471 --> 00:05:35.668
Key outcomes: checkout migration (minus 60% failed orders), 35 lakh rupees per year cloud savings. mentored 4 engineers (2 promoted).

112
00:05:35.948 --> 00:05:41.836
Market data: current fixed pay is ~15% below the range for Senior Engineer in.

113
00:05:42.116 --> 00:05:43.119
Bangalore.

114
00:05:43.399 --> 00:05:45.468
The rest of the template is on the page.

115
00:05:48.318 --> 00:05:49.278
Part 5.

116
00:05:49.558 --> 00:05:51.905
Phrases that help and phrases that hurt.

117
00:05:53.155 --> 00:05:54.520
Here is a quick table:

118
00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:56.421
Avoid, and Use instead.

119
00:05:56.701 --> 00:05:59.027
This is unfair, everyone else got more.:

120
00:05:59.307 --> 00:06:01.909
Can you help me understand how this was decided?

121
00:06:02.189 --> 00:06:03.896
I'll leave if this doesn't change.:

122
00:06:04.176 --> 00:06:05.840
I want to build my career here.

123
00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:07.123
What would it take?

124
00:06:07.403 --> 00:06:09.493
My friend at X got 40%.:

125
00:06:09.773 --> 00:06:12.291
Market data for my role shows a range of...

126
00:06:12.571 --> 00:06:15.088
Silent acceptance, then complaining later:

127
00:06:15.368 --> 00:06:18.291
Can I take a day to think about this and come back to you?

128
00:06:18.571 --> 00:06:20.320
Discussing your hike with teammates:

129
00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:23.992
Keep your numbers private; it's usually company policy too.

130
00:06:25.342 --> 00:06:26.281
Part 6.

131
00:06:26.561 --> 00:06:28.651
For team leads delivering appraisals.

132
00:06:29.901 --> 00:06:34.957
Know the numbers before the meeting: the band, the reasoning, what's flexible and what isn't.

133
00:06:35.237 --> 00:06:37.413
Lead with the outcome, then the reasoning.

134
00:06:37.693 --> 00:06:40.168
Don't make people wait 20 minutes for the number.

135
00:06:40.448 --> 00:06:43.051
Don't blame management for numbers you supported.

136
00:06:43.331 --> 00:06:45.400
If you fought for more, you can say so.

137
00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:46.768
Allow emotion:

138
00:06:47.048 --> 00:06:49.117
I understand this is disappointing.

139
00:06:49.397 --> 00:06:50.400
Take your time.

140
00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:53.901
Never promise what you can't guarantee (Next year for sure).

141
00:06:55.251 --> 00:06:57.939
End with a clear growth plan and a check-in date.

142
00:06:59.289 --> 00:07:00.228
Part 7.

143
00:07:00.508 --> 00:07:01.895
Practice conversation:

144
00:07:02.175 --> 00:07:03.732
Hike below expectation.

145
00:07:04.982 --> 00:07:07.009
Here is how the conversation goes.

146
00:07:07.289 --> 00:07:09.379
Your hike this year is 7%.

147
00:07:09.659 --> 00:07:11.323
Thank you for explaining.

148
00:07:11.603 --> 00:07:15.038
Honestly, it's lower than I expected given my rating.

149
00:07:15.318 --> 00:07:16.555
I understand.

150
00:07:16.835 --> 00:07:18.563
The budget was tight this year.

151
00:07:18.843 --> 00:07:23.451
Market data shows I'm about 15% below the range for my role.

152
00:07:23.731 --> 00:07:26.654
Is there a possibility of a market correction?

153
00:07:28.004 --> 00:07:29.604
The conversation continues.

154
00:07:29.884 --> 00:07:32.167
That's not something I can approve myself.

155
00:07:32.447 --> 00:07:34.260
Could you take it to HR?

156
00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:37.057
I'm happy to put the details in writing.

157
00:07:37.337 --> 00:07:39.599
Yes, send me an email and I'll forward it.

158
00:07:39.879 --> 00:07:40.817
Thank you.

159
00:07:41.097 --> 00:07:45.535
And if nothing changes now, could we agree on a review in six months?

160
00:07:45.815 --> 00:07:47.052
That's fair.

161
00:07:47.332 --> 00:07:48.441
I'll note it.

162
00:07:49.791 --> 00:07:51.327
That's the end of this lesson.

163
00:07:51.607 --> 00:07:56.279
Pick one script from it, say it out loud three times, and use it for real today.

164
00:07:56.559 --> 00:08:02.661
For extra practice, paste the Master Prompt into an AI assistant and role-play the conversation.

